   {:expectation :diverges,
    :predicate :keyword-vs-class,
    :note
    "Oracle returns a Class object; mino\n                                  returns the analogous keyword tag."},
   :category-id :type-system,
   :title "type returns a keyword, not a Java class",
   :doc-link "/coming-from-clojure#types",
   :rationale
   "No JVM classes. mino's type system is keyword-\n                    tagged (:vector, :map, :list, :symbol, ...).\n                    type reports the concrete tag and, unlike class,\n                    honours :type metadata when present. Dispatch\n                    logic that pattern-matches on classes must use\n                    the keyword tags instead.",
   :id :type-as-keyword}
  {:dialect-example "(class nil) ;=> nil, (class [1 2 3]) ;=> :vector",
   :since "v0.1.0",
   :affected [clojure.core/class],
   :clojure-example
   "(class nil) ;=> nil, (class [1 2 3]) ;=> clojure.lang.PersistentVector",
   :behavior
   {:expectation :diverges,
    :predicate :keyword-vs-class,
    :note
    "nil yields nil (a true match); other\n                                  values yield the analogous keyword\n                                  or record-type symbol where the\n                                  oracle returns a Class object."},
   :category-id :type-system,
   :title "class is a concrete-tag function, not a JVM class",
   :rationale
   "There are no JVM classes, so class is a real\n                    concrete-tag function rather than an alias of\n                    type: nil yields nil, a record yields the\n                    record's type symbol, and every other value\n                    yields its type keyword. class ignores :type\n                    metadata, which is what distinguishes it from\n                    type. Code that branches on a host Class must use\n                    the keyword/symbol tags instead.",
   :id :class-of-non-jvm}
